            <ArticleTitle>The suicides of The Metamorphoses.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>22-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/10398562.2010.506218</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this paper was to explore the attitude toward and motives for suicide recorded in Ovid's The Metamorphoses, a document that has influenced Western culture.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">The translation by Horace Gregory was examined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Twenty-five mentions of suicidal thoughts or actions were identified, mainly by humans but also by a god, a nymph and a centaur. Fifteen suicides by humans were identified. The motives were predicaments including the loss of a loved individual or status and the threat of murder or plague. There was one instance of intolerable physical pain and one of defiance in the face of intimidation. While there are mentions of madness, there is no mention of madness leading to suicide. There was no censure of suicide.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Suicide in Western culture appears to have roots in ancient times.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
